body:
Interesting notes:
1. If You use `var typed_dict: Dictionary[ColorRect, String] = {}`, and then You assign the `PremiumColorRect` (so `var rect := PremiumColorRect.new()`), You don't get the issue with editor. There is a chance that it just struggles to get some sort of reference or size, or something else of this sort, that all built-in classes have, but custom classes don't get.
```GDScript
extends Control

var typed_dict: Dictionary[ColorRect, String] = {}

func add_rect() -> void:
	var rect := PremiumColorRect.new()

	typed_dict[rect] = "ASDF"

	add_child(rect)
	print(rect is PremiumColorRect)

func _ready() -> void:
	add_rect()
```
2. Unlike in #105441, trying to print the dictionary returns correct values.
```GDScript
extends Control

var typed_dict: Dictionary[PremiumColorRect, String] = {}

func add_rect() -> void:
	var rect := PremiumColorRect.new()

	typed_dict[rect] = "ASDF"

	add_child(rect)
	print(typed_dict)

func _ready() -> void:
	add_rect()
```
